{"args":["--project","issue-viewer","append","workflow/local-ci-validate-integrated-main-after-every-merge-and-report-failures","--section","Idea","--body","**In short:** once a project opts in with `ci:` in workflow.yaml, every move of its integration branch (local `main` here, `master` in RogueTech) starts a run of the configured commands in a persistent CI worktree. The result goes into `.ci/` in the project root. A red result is reported where people look: the Status page and `/status.md`, a `ci-red` chip on the issues merged since the last green run, a `ci:` comment on each of them, a nudge to the live lead, and one line in `issue-cli ci status`, `start` and the dispatch prompt. Agents never wait for it.\n\n**Why it is needed:** agents run `make validate` on their own branch before the fast-forward. Two branches that are green on their own can be red together, nobody runs the slow checks, and when the lead runs them by hand nobody else hears the result. Origin moves only at release time, so GitHub-side CI would find a break days late. RogueTech's D-6 rules out external CI anyway.\n\n**Edge cases and constraints**\n- **Coalescing:** runs are serialized per project root by `.ci/lock` (O_EXCL; holds pid, sha and start time). The runner loops until it has tested the current head, so three merges during a run give one more run, of the newest head. A lock whose pid is dead counts as stale and is replaced.\n- **Server restart:** the run is a detached `issue-cli ci run` (its own tmux window), so restarting the viewer does not kill it. On start, the watcher runs CI when the head differs from the last recorded sha and no live lock exists.\n- **Server and `ci watch` both running:** both may try to start a run; the lock lets only one through, and the loser prints \"already running at \u003csha\u003e\".\n- **First run ever (no green yet):** no blame, because the whole history would be blamed. The Status page and the lead nudge still report it.\n- **Branch rewound or force-moved** (head is not a descendant of the last green): blame is counted from their merge-base. If nothing can be attributed, only the lead is nudged.\n- **Timeout:** the process group is killed. The run is red with `\u003ccmd\u003e (timeout after 20m)`.\n- **Infrastructure error** (worktree broken, git fails, command not found to start): the state is `error`, not red. Nobody is blamed. The Status page says \"CI can't run: \u003creason\u003e; `issue-cli ci reset`\".\n- **Still red:** later red runs do not comment again or re-nudge unless the failing command changes. The lead gets one more nudge when main goes back to green.\n- **Commits that are not merges** (lead hotfix on main, `git pull` of a cloud PR): the watcher watches the ref, not the merge command, so these trigger runs too. Blame uses branch tips in the range plus `Merge branch '…'` / `Merge pull request … from …` subjects. A commit made straight on main is listed by sha.\n- **Environment:** commands run under `sh -c` in `.worktrees/.ci`, under `nice -n \u003cci.nice\u003e` (default 10, D-8). They get `CI=true`, and `ISSUE_VIEWER_*` and `TMUX` are removed, so issue-cli inside tests never thinks it is an agent and never reaches the real tmux.\n- **Worktree hygiene:** each run does `checkout --detach --force \u003csha\u003e` plus `git clean -fd` (no `-x`). Ignored build output (bin/obj, `.godot`, Go cache) survives, so builds are incremental. `.worktrees/.ci` is detached: it must not show up as an open branch, an agent or a hot-file source (checked and tested).\n- **Two projects on one repo root:** the lock is per root, so CI runs once.\n- **Storage:** `.ci/` writes its own `.gitignore` (`*`), so no repo has to ignore it. Runs older than the newest 50 are pruned.\n- **No `ci:` block:** no watcher goroutine, no Status row, no marker, no line in start/dispatch. `issue-cli ci status` prints `ci: not configured` with a hint and exits 0.\n\n**Not included**\n- GitHub Actions (`workflow/run-make-validate-in-ci-on-every-push`), a remote or GPU runner, auto-revert (the lead decides).\n- The \"Ready to cut vX\" release gate, deferred to `ui/status-page-shows-what-waits-to-be-released` (D-10). Here, `ci status` exposes the state and the lead brief says not to push while red.\n- Turning CI on in RogueTech (their lead adds `ci: run: [make check]`, D-7).\n- Health trends over CI durations. Run records carry per-command durations for `workflow/health-check-speed-ux-test-timing-tree-and-ci-durations` to read later.\n- A `workflow upgrade` catalog feature for `ci:`."],"pid":3175772,"ppid":3175770,"ts":"2026-10-22T14:13:48Z"}
{"args":["--project","issue-viewer","append","workflow/local-ci-validate-integrated-main-after-every-merge-and-report-failures","--section","Design","--body","### Approach\n\nBuilt in four commits, in the lead's order (D-7). Each one is usable on its own.\n\n**1. Config, runner, `ci run / status / reset`** (usable by hand)\n\n`tracker.CIConfig` is a project-wide `ci:` field on `WorkflowConfig`, like `workbench:`, so system and type overlays cannot override it. `desc` tags feed `workflow schema` and lint.\n```yaml\nci:\n  branch: main          # default: the branch checked out in the main checkout; detached HEAD → error \"set ci.branch\"\n  timeout: 20m          # whole run; default 10m\n  nice: 10              # default 10, 0 = off (D-8)\n  on_red: mark          # mark (default, D-9) | file_issue\n  run:                  # in order; the first failure stops the run\n    - make validate                   # a plain string = {cmd, tier: default}\n    - cmd: make ux-test\n      tier: slow                      # not run on merge; `ci run --tier slow` runs default + slow (D-8)\n      failures: '--- FAIL: (\\S+)'     # optional; capture group 1 = test name for the one-liner\n```\nLint rejects: an empty `run`, a bad duration, an unknown `on_red`, a regex that doesn't compile, and a `failures` regex without a group.\n\nA new package, `internal/ci`, used by both issue-cli and the server:\n- `Worktree(root)`: on first use runs `git worktree add --detach .worktrees/.ci \u003csha\u003e`, then reuses it with `checkout --detach --force \u003csha\u003e` and `clean -fd`. `Reset` removes it (`worktree remove --force`) and adds it again.\n- `Lock(root)`: `.ci/lock`, O_EXCL, JSON {pid, sha, tier, started}. A lock whose pid is dead is stale.\n- `Run(root, cfg, tier)`: under the lock, loops `head := rev-parse refs/heads/\u003cbranch\u003e` until `head == last tested`. Each command runs as `nice -n N sh -c \u003ccmd\u003e` in its own process group with a filtered env. Output is tee'd to stdout (so the tmux window shows it live) and to `.ci/runs/\u003csha\u003e.log`. Timeout kills the group.\n- **Record** `.ci/runs/\u003csha\u003e.json` (slow tier: `\u003csha\u003e.slow.json`): sha, subject, branch, tier, started, finished, `state` green|red|error, `commands[]` {cmd, tier, exit, duration_ms, failures[]}, `failed`, `log`, `log_tail` (last 40 lines), `range_from` (last green sha), `merged[]` {branch, slug, sha}.\n- **State** `.ci/state.json` is the only file the server reads: branch, `last_sha`, `state`, `green_sha`, `red_since`, `failed`, `failures`, `blamed[]` slugs, `red_issue`, `log_tail`, `updated`. It is written atomically (tmp file + rename). Slow-tier results are kept under `slow:` and never drive red, blame or nudges: whoever runs the slow tier asks for it and watches it.\n- **Blame:** the range is `green_sha..sha`, from the merge-base when the branch was rewound. It collects (a) `refs/heads/work/*` tips that are ancestors of sha and not of green_sha, mapped to issues with the existing `tracker.FindIssue`, and (b) merge-commit subjects in the range naming a branch. Commits made straight on main are listed by sha.\n\nCLI (`cmd/issue-cli/cmd_ci.go`, registered like the other commands, parsed with `parseFlags` for telemetry):\n- `ci status [--json] [--tier slow]` prints one line first: `main green at abc1234 (12m ago)`, `main red since def5678: make validate: TestFoo, TestBar (+3)`, `main: running at abc1234 (3m) · last green def5678`, or `main: CI can't run: \u003creason\u003e — issue-cli ci reset`. The last 5 runs follow. `--json` dumps the state and the recent runs.\n- `ci run [--tier slow] [--detach]`: foreground by default. If a run is already going, it prints `already running at \u003csha\u003e (pid N); it tests the newest head when done` and exits 0. Exit code: 0 green, 1 red or error. `--detach` starts the same command in tmux window `ci-\u003cproject\u003e`, in the project's shared session when it has one, otherwise as session `ci-\u003cproject\u003e`. Without tmux it falls back to a `setsid` background process writing to `.ci/runner.log`.\n- `ci reset`: refuses while locked, then rebuilds the worktree.\n- `ci watch [--interval 5s]`: a foreground loop that runs `ci run --detach` when the head ≠ last_sha and no live lock exists. For running without the viewer.\n\n**2. Watcher in the server** (`ci_watch.go`)\n\n`main` starts one goroutine per configured project with a distinct root. Every 5 s it runs `git rev-parse refs/heads/\u003cbranch\u003e`; one cheap call. When the head ≠ `state.last_sha` and no live lock exists, it exec's `issue-cli --config \u003ccfg\u003e --project \u003cname\u003e ci run --detach`, with `issue-cli` from PATH. That also covers startup (\"picks up where it left off\"). The workflow config is re-read on each tick, so adding or removing `ci:` takes effect without a restart. The runner process is not a child the server waits on, so restarting the server never kills a run.\n\n**3. Reporting** (done by the runner at the end of a run, so it also works without the viewer)\n- **green→red, or red with a new failing command:** each blamed issue gets a comment `ci: local CI red on main at \u003csha\u003e: \\`\u003ccmd\u003e\\` failed (\u003cfirst failures\u003e). Log: .ci/runs/\u003csha\u003e.log. issue-cli ci status` (source `ci`). The live lead gets `[from ci] local CI red on main at \u003csha\u003e: \u003ccmd\u003e — merged since green: a, b. issue-cli ci status` through the existing `agentrun.Paste`.\n- **red→green:** the lead is nudged with `[from ci] main green again at \u003csha\u003e`. Blame and the chips clear.\n- **`on_red: file_issue`** (opt-in): on green→red it creates one `bugfix` issue, `Fix red CI on main: \u003ccmd\u003e`, in the system of the first blamed issue, else no system. The body holds the range, the merged issues and the log path. It is stored as `red_issue`, linked from the `ci:` comments, and gets a progress comment on each later red run. When main is green again it gets a comment, and is left open for whoever ships the fix.\n- **Card marker `ci-red`** (`markers.go`), red tone: `CI red · \u003ccmd\u003e`, with the commit range as tooltip. It is read from `state.blamed` per request, nothing is written to frontmatter, and it shows on done cards too.\n- **Status page:** a \"Local CI\" row: green dot `main green at abc1234 · 12m ago`, or red `red since def5678 · make validate` with the first failures, the log tail in a `\u003cdetails\u003e`, and the blamed issues as links. A red state counts as one owner-attention item (sidebar count and the top of \"Waiting on you\"), as a new `WaitingItem` kind `ci` with no issue. `/status.md` gets a `Local CI:` line, and while red, the tail in a fenced block.\n- `internal/testids` gets the new ids (`status-ci`, `marker-ci-red`).\n\n**4. Turn it on here, plus prompts**\n- This repo's workflow.yaml: `ci: run: [make validate, {cmd: make ux-test, tier: slow}]`, timeout 20m.\n- Bundled templates: a commented-out `ci:` example, and one sentence in the shipping prompt (below).\n- Lead brief (`tracker/lead_brief.go`): run `issue-cli ci status` before any push; never push a release while main is red; on a `[from ci]` nudge, decide between revert, fix forward or dispatch.\n\n**Statuses, transitions, overlays:** none change. No migration: without `ci:` everything behaves as today.\n\n### Dependencies\n- Code reused: `tracker.WorkflowConfig` and schema/lint, `tracker.FindIssue` (branch→issue), `tracker.AddComment`, the issue create path (for `file_issue`), `agentrun` (`Target`, `SharedSession`, `CreateTmux`, `Paste`, `IsLive`, `LeadSessionName`), `status.go`/`attention.go`/`markers.go`, `cmd_start.go`, `tracker/dispatch_prompt.go`, `tracker/lead_brief.go`.\n- Tools: git (required), tmux (optional, `setsid` fallback), `issue-cli` on PATH for the server to spawn runs. `make install` is already in the shipping routine. The server logs a warning once if issue-cli is missing.\n- Issues: none block this. `ui/status-page-shows-what-waits-to-be-released` later reads `ci status --json` for its release gate (D-10). `workflow/health-check-speed-ux-test-timing-tree-and-ci-durations` reads per-command durations from `.ci/runs`.\n\n### Overlap with existing features\n- **Shipping step 3, `make validate` on the branch after `git merge main`:** this is per branch, before the merge. It misses breaks between parallel merges, a lead hotfix or a pulled cloud PR, skips slow checks, and tells nobody. CI keeps it (the cheap first filter) and adds the integration check.\n- **`command_succeeds` validator** (`allow_shell`): it runs synchronously at a transition, for one issue, and blocks the agent. It is the wrong place for an integration check nobody should wait on. `ci:` does not require `allow_shell`: the block is its own explicit opt-in, and the commands are the project's own make targets.\n- **`worktree_setup`** is a one-shot bootstrap of a dispatch worktree. It is unrelated, apart from the persistent-worktree idea.\n- **`make playtest-check` / `ux-test`** are what CI runs, not a runner.\n- **GitHub Actions issue** (`workflow/run-make-validate-in-ci-on-every-push`): remote and on push only. Origin moves at release, so it would report days late. Owner and RogueTech D-6 chose local.\n- **Workbench metrics files:** read-only probe results, a possible later sink for CI durations, not a runner.\n\nShrinking considered: dropping `ci watch` (the server watcher alone) saves about 30 lines, but the lead asked for a viewer-less path and it shares the poll loop, so it stays. Dropping `file_issue` follows the D-9 default, but RogueTech needs it, and it is one function behind a flag, so it stays.\n\n### Discoverability (CLI output first, prompt text second)\n- `issue-cli start \u003cslug\u003e` and the dispatch prompt (built by issue-cli, so every project gets it) print the one-liner when `ci:` is set: `Local CI: main red since def5678: make validate: TestFoo — inherited; not caused by your branch`. With green, one short line; without `ci:`, nothing.\n- `issue-cli transition \u003cslug\u003e --to done` and the shipping checklist output print after the fast-forward: `Local CI runs on main at \u003csha\u003e; don't wait — if it goes red on your merge, the lead may re-open the issue (issue-cli ci status)`.\n- `issue-cli help` lists `ci`. `ci status` without config gives the hint `add a ci: block to workflow.yaml (see docs/workflow.md#local-ci)`. `workflow schema` documents every key.\n- Prompts: one sentence in the shipping prompt (this repo's workflow.yaml and the bundled templates), and the lead brief lines above.\n- Docs: docs/workflow.md (Local CI section and schema), docs/agent-dispatch.md and docs/patterns/lead-session.md (nudges, the push rule), docs/roadmap.md Releases (CI green before a cut), docs/CLI/overview.md (`ci` commands and the output contract), CLAUDE.md project-structure lines.\n\n### Tests (AC6)\n- `internal/ci`: real git in `t.TempDir()` repos and fake commands (`sh -c 'exit 1'`, `sleep`) for green, red, timeout, error, coalescing (commits during a run → one more run of the newest head), the stale lock, blame (ff, `--no-ff`, rewound branch, first run), the worktree kept between runs (an ignored file survives) and `reset`.\n- Server: the watcher with a fake spawner and fake rev-parse; Status row green, red and absent; `/status.md`; the `ci-red` marker; no `ci:` → nothing shows and no goroutine starts.\n- CLI: `ci status` text and `--json`, not configured, `run` while locked, the lead nudge with a fake `pasteIntoSession`, and the one-liners in `start` and the dispatch prompt."],"pid":3181367,"ppid":3181365,"ts":"2026-10-22T14:14:41Z"}
{"args":["--project","issue-viewer","append","workflow/local-ci-validate-integrated-main-after-every-merge-and-report-failures","--section","Acceptance Criteria","--body","- [ ] The CI worktree persists between runs (ignored build output survives); `issue-cli ci reset` rebuilds it; runs survive a viewer restart and a restart picks up an untested head; `ci watch` works without the viewer\n- [ ] Per-command `tier: slow` is skipped on merge and run by `ci run --tier slow`; commands run under `nice` in tmux window `ci-\u003cproject\u003e` with a per-project lock\n- [ ] `issue-cli start` and the dispatch prompt print the one-line CI state (with `failures:` test names) when `ci:` is set; `transition --to done` says CI runs asynchronously\n- [ ] `ci.on_red: file_issue` files one bugfix issue on green→red and updates it while red; default `mark` files nothing"],"pid":3182813,"ppid":3182811,"ts":"2026-10-22T14:14:53Z"}
